x86_64-linux  two recorded rules, no new one. Every constructor here is inline, so clang writes only the form a use asks for and cxx1 writes both of Itanium's - docs/CONFORMANCE.md, "An inline member function is a strong symbol" - which is the six C1 forms cxx1 has and clang does not. And clang guards `new M` and `new P(1)`: if the constructor throws, the memory has to be freed, so it emits the cleanup - _Unwind_Resume, the personality, _ZdlPv - where cxx1's failed new terminates, docs/CONFORMANCE.md again. The Windows comparison is where the clone of a `++n` operand used to spell the C name. It does not agree exactly - an earlier version of this line said it did, from clang's Microsoft output rather than from cl - see this case's .nocl
arm64-darwin  same two rules: Base's C1 is cxx1's extra, and _ZdlPv with __Unwind_Resume are clang's new-cleanup, which Darwin's clang emits for `new` as Linux's does
